Description: Profiles tabular data and measures its quality. Discovers
    structural patterns, implicit types, mixed date formats and reusable
    findings about potential data issues; defines quality frameworks with
    user-declared dimensions, reusable metrics with explicit granularity and
    typed aggregation; keeps versioned histories and detects structural drift;
    writes self-contained HyperText Markup Language reports; and produces
    editable cleaning plans that apply only the actions the user selects, with
    an audit log. The general model follows Batini and Scannapieco (2016)
    <doi:10.1007/978-3-319-24106-7>. The package implements version 1.6 of the
    data quality management framework for digital government of the Uruguayan
    Agency for Electronic Government and Information and Knowledge Society,
    AGESIC (2020) <https://www.gub.uy/agencia-gobierno-electronico-sociedad-informacion-conocimiento/comunicacion/publicaciones/marco-trabajo-para-gestion-calidad-datos>;
    an optional taxonomy of the fifteen characteristics of the standard 25012
    of the International Organization for Standardization and the
    International Electrotechnical Commission, ISO/IEC (2008)
    <https://www.iso.org/standard/35736.html>; and the four levels and nineteen
    principles of the national quality assurance framework of the United
    Nations (2019) <https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/dataquality/>, as
    adapted for Latin America and the Caribbean by the Statistical Conference
    of the Americas of the Economic Commission for Latin America and the
    Caribbean, CEA-CEPAL (2022)
    <https://repositorio.cepal.org/handle/11362/47464>. AGESIC, CEA and CEPAL
    are the Spanish acronyms of these institutions.
